[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSection 2 — Testing and tooling[0m

  This repo has no test runner, no linter, and zero dependencies. That's worth deciding now rather than after the code
  exists, because it's cheapest before.

  [1mWhat's worth testing.[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m holds the only real logic: round-tripping an id, returning [38;5;153mnull[39m when nothing is
  stored, clearing, and the fallback path when [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws. That last one is the whole reason the try/catch
  exists, and it's the case nobody reproduces by hand. To make it testable, [38;5;153msession.js[39m reads [38;5;153mglobalThis.localStorage[39m at
  call time rather than capturing a reference at import — so a test can substitute a throwing stub. That's a small
  design constraint the tests impose on the module, and I'd rather state it than have it look arbitrary later.

  [1mWhat I'd use.[0m Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runner. It keeps the repo at zero dependencies, which seems worth preserving
  here, and it handles ES modules natively. Node has no [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, but since the module resolves it at call time, the
  tests just define one.

  [1mLinting[0m would mean adding eslint and prettier — the first dependencies this repo has ever had, plus config files, to
  police roughly 60 lines of JavaScript. I don't think that trade is worth it yet, but it's your call. End-to-end and
  fuzz testing I'd skip outright at this size; I'm not offering them.
